We want to measure ‘asset visibility’ as a core success metric for our customers. How would you go about 
defining this metric precisely? What would you include in the definition? What potential pitfalls would you 
watch out for?

First, I'd want to make sure we define “asset visibility” in a way that's meaningful to customers. 
For them, visibility means knowing where their assets are and what condition they're in, reliably and 
in near real-time.

I’d define Asset Visibility as the percentage of active assets with accurate, recent location and 
condition data available in the system.

Specifically, it could be measured as:

Number of active assets with valid location + condition updates within the last 30 minutes, divided by 
total active assets.

We'd also want to clearly define “active assets” (those expected to be in transit or monitored) and set 
agreed-upon freshness thresholds with stakeholders.

Pitfalls to watch out for include:

Overly strict freshness windows making the metric look bad even when acceptable.

Sensor outages or network delays skewing results.

False positives from bad data quality.

Different customer use cases (e.g. in-transit vs stored assets needing different update frequencies).*





